{"timestamp": "2025-07-08T11:47:46.334772", "request_id": "8135870d-1206-4edb-8b6a-406753cf92b6", "nickname": "VictorOrban", "model": "openai", "summary": "The study found that even the simplest baseline model, which averaged the training examples, outperformed the foundation models scGPT and scFoundation. Furthermore, basic machine learning models incorporating biologically meaningful features, such as Random Forest Regressor with Gene Ontology features, outperformed the foundation models by a large margin. This indicates that the simplest predictor, the Train Mean model, was stronger than the foundation models for predicting post-treatment RNA-seq vectors of perturbed cells in the contexts evaluated in this study."}
{"timestamp": "2025-07-08T11:47:47.257563", "request_id": "8135870d-1206-4edb-8b6a-406753cf92b6", "nickname": "VictorOrban", "model": "perplexity", "summary": "According to the talk summarized in the provided document, the strongest model highlighted is the **Random Forest Regressor** that incorporates biological prior knowledge using Gene Ontology (GO) features. This model consistently **outperformed both foundation models (scGPT, scFoundation) and simpler baselines** across multiple benchmark datasets for post-perturbation RNA-seq prediction. The results also revealed that even a simple \u201cTrain Mean\u201d baseline model performed better than the foundation models in many cases. The foundation models, despite being state-of-the-art in some publications, did not surpass traditional machine learning models when evaluated using robust benchmarks focused on differential expression. This challenges the perceived superiority of large foundation models for this specific task under current benchmark conditions."}
